body:
Summary
In src/transcript.ts:103 the Ed25519 signature covers only `room|nonce|line`:
`${room}|${nonce}|${line}`
`seq` and `ts` are venue metadata parsed in src/transcript.ts:131 via `Date.parse(message.ts)` and then used as `nowMs` in every deadline guard in src/machine.ts:121,158,178,192.

A file supplier (payer handing `deal.jsonl` to an arbiter, same model as #93) can rewrite `ts` without breaking any signature.

The Problem
- Honest deal: `reveal` at T+30m (< refundAfterMs 2h) → `claimed`, `refund` after → rejected. Correct.
- Same 5 signed records, only `reveal.ts` edited to T+2h+10s (> refundAfterMs), `refund.ts` unchanged → `reveal` now rejected “refund window is open”, `refund` accepted → `refunded`. Every `verifyTranscriptRecord` still `ok==true`, `audit-export.mjs` exits 0, only `ts` string changed.
- This is the separate `ts`-malleability you reserved in #93 scope notes (“holder edits ts to move a record across a deadline, kept out so the two can be judged apart”). No open issue covers it - #93 is gap/row-order, #78 is nonce >2^53.

Reproduction on main @ 5cc4ab9:

    const {makeOffer, makeAccept, generateHashLock, dealRoom, OFFER_ROOM, foldTranscript, verifyTranscriptRecord, encodeFrame} = await import("./dist/index.js");
    // 5 genuine records, all verifyTranscriptRecord.ok === true
    const T0=1780000000000;
    const offer=makeOffer({from:payerDid, role:"payer", lock:"hash", amount:"1000000", asset:"FLOP", rails:["paper"], claimByMs:T0+3600000, refundAfterMs:T0+7200000, expiresMs:T0+600000});
    const {preimage,hash}=generateHashLock();
    const accept=makeAccept(offer,{from:payeeDid, statement:hash});
    const room=dealRoom(accept.contract);
    // honest
    foldTranscript([offerRec,acceptRec,lockRec,revealRec@T+1800000,refundRec@T+7205000]) // => claimed
    // tampered: same records, reveal.timestampMs = T+7210000
    verifyTranscriptRecord(tamperedReveal) // => ok true (ts not signed)
    foldTranscript([offerRec,acceptRec,lockRec,tamperedReveal,refundRec]) // => refunded

Proposed Fix
In src/transcript.ts:236 `foldTranscript` should not treat `ts`/`seq` as authenticated. Add `warnings: string[]` to `TranscriptFoldResult`:
- per-room `gap detected (seq 1->3)`, `seq not strictly increasing`, `timestamp goes backwards`
- generic note when any deadline frame (`accept`/`lock`/`reveal`/`refund`) is present: “timestamps and seq are venue metadata, not covered by signature - verify settlement on the rail”

Do not refuse to fold (partial windows/post-reap are legitimate), just surface it. `examples/audit-export.mjs` and `mcp/src/tools.ts:tclk_apply_transcript` forward `warnings`; audit exits 1 on gap/ordering/backwards. `SPEC.md:62` documents that `ts`/`seq` being unsigned means a supplier can rewrite `ts` to flip deadlines - transcript is coordination, not settlement proof.

PR `fix/transcript-timestamp-auth` implements this - no wire change.

**Checked against `main` @ `5cc4ab9`** — built locally, and the reproduction below is a plain
script against `dist/`, as in #36.

`foldTranscript` (`src/transcript.ts:236`) authenticates every record and then applies them in
whatever order the array arrived in. It never checks `seq` monotonicity, never checks for a gap,
and never checks that the stream is complete. The only `seq` reference in the fold is
`seq: record?.seq ?? -1` (`src/transcript.ts:241`), which labels a verdict and validates nothing.

`examples/audit-export.mjs:40` folds `[handshake.offer, handshake.accept, ...deal]`, where `deal`
is `parseTranscriptExport` over the file's lines in file order, with no sort. So the party that
supplies `deal.jsonl` chooses the fold order — and which rows exist at all.

## What I observe

Five genuine records from one deal. The payee revealed inside the window; the payer later posted a
`refund` that the live machine rejected, but the venue stored and signed it anyway. All five
signatures verify in every case below, and no timestamp is altered — this does not depend on
`ts` being unsigned.

```
contract : 0x3fe76d7554df72196bb9c8dd1c6d4c64d532af08bfe2838ccd777d27d5fdc122
deal room: mb-p-tclk-3fe76d7554df7219

A. honest order (what actually happened)
  every signature verifies : true
  seq order as folded      : 1,2,1,2,3
  [0] offer  seq=1 ok=true
  [1] accept seq=2 ok=true
  [2] lock   seq=1 ok=true
  [3] reveal seq=2 ok=true
  [4] refund seq=3 ok=false refund in status claimed
  => status: claimed

B. same five records, two lines swapped
  every signature verifies : true
  seq order as folded      : 1,2,1,3,2
  [0] offer  seq=1 ok=true
  [1] accept seq=2 ok=true
  [2] lock   seq=1 ok=true
  [3] refund seq=3 ok=true
  [4] reveal seq=2 ok=false reveal in status refunded
  => status: refunded

C. same records, the reveal row deleted
  every signature verifies : true
  seq order as folded      : 1,2,1,3
  [0] offer  seq=1 ok=true
  [1] accept seq=2 ok=true
  [2] lock   seq=1 ok=true
  [3] refund seq=3 ok=true
  => status: refunded
```

**C is the sharp one.** B produces the wrong status but still prints a `BAD` line an attentive
reader could catch. C prints **no `BAD` line at all** — every verdict is `ok`, the fold reaches a
terminal status, and `audit-export.mjs:52` exits `0`. The only trace is the deal room's `seq`
running `1,3`, which the tool prints and nothing checks.

A `refund` at `refundAfterMs + 5s` against a `locked` contract is a perfectly legal transition.
Removing the row that would have made it illegal is the entire attack.

## What a hostile counterparty gets

Per CONTRIBUTING's "say what a hostile counterparty gets from it, or say 'nothing'":

A payer who received the work, and whose payee genuinely claimed, can hand any third party — an
arbiter under §8.1, a reputation layer consuming `receipt` frames, a future dispute process — a
`deal.jsonl` that reproduces as `refunded` under the project's own shipped auditor, with every
signature intact and a zero exit code. The payee's counter-evidence is a *different* file, and
nothing in the format lets a third party tell which of the two is complete.

Symmetrically, a payee can drop a `lock` row to make a funded deal look unfunded.

This needs no forged signature, no key, no clock manipulation, and no cooperation from the venue.
It needs a text editor.

## Which is wrong, `SPEC.md` or the code

I treated **the code as correct about what it does, and the surrounding claims as overselling it.**

- `foldTranscript`'s own doc comment — "Authenticate and fold records in the supplied order" — is
  accurate and even says "supplied order" out loud.
- SPEC §2 says an offline reader "trusts the export file" for `seq` and `ts`. That is a statement
  about *values*, and it is honest. It does not say the reader also trusts the file for
  *completeness*, which is the stronger property the fold actually needs.
- README calls the fold the thing that "authenticates every record" and `examples/` presents
  `audit-export.mjs` as an "offline audit of a finished deal". Both are true about signatures and
  false about outcomes.

So: no wire-format change is implied, and no golden vector moves. What is missing is a guard, or
an explicit precondition, or both.

## Candidate fixes

**A — the fold hard-refuses a non-contiguous stream.** Cheapest to state, and I do not recommend
it. SPEC §2 says rooms are a ring reaped after 7 idle days and that `seq` restarts if a room is
recreated, so a legitimately windowed or post-reap export would start failing, and the fold cannot
tell a censored gap from a retention boundary. It would also compound #61, where the room binding
already rejects live deals.

**B — the fold sorts by `(room, seq)` itself rather than trusting arrival order.** Closes case B.
Does nothing for case C, which is the one with no `BAD` line. Worth doing, not sufficient alone.

**C — report contiguity instead of enforcing it.** Add a per-room gap marker to `steps`, and a
`contiguous: boolean` (or `gaps: Array<{room, after, before}>`) to `TranscriptFoldResult`. Callers
that legitimately hold a partial window keep working; `audit-export.mjs` prints a warning and
exits non-zero; nothing is refused that works today. This is the one I'd propose.

**D — document the precondition.** State in SPEC §2 and in `foldTranscript`'s doc that the fold
reproduces an outcome only over a *complete, per-room-ordered* stream, and have
`audit-export.mjs` say so in its output. Cheap, and needed regardless of which of A–C lands,
because a reader of the README currently has no way to know.

**Proposed: B + C + D.** Sort what can be sorted, report what cannot be verified, and stop
implying the fold is evidence when it is a replay. I'm happy to open a PR for that shape if you
agree with it — flagging first per CONTRIBUTING's "discuss a change to the state machine in an
issue first", since `TranscriptFoldResult` is public surface.

## Scope notes

- **Independent of the unsigned-`ts` question.** Every timestamp above is genuine. There is a
  separate, sharper issue where a holder edits `ts` to move a record across a deadline; I have
  kept it out of this report so the two can be judged apart.
- **Not #61.** That is the room binding rejecting live deals. This is the fold accepting a stream
  it should not.
- **Related to #23** only in that #23's fix is what made the fold depend on per-record venue
  metadata in the first place.
- **Not #83.** `findContractHandshake` not re-deriving the contract id is a separate hole on the
  offer/accept half; this one is entirely in the deal-room half, and persists even if #83's fix
  (PR #84) lands.

## Reproduction

`node repro.mjs` from the repo root, after `pnpm -r --include-workspace-root build`. Contract id
and deal room vary per run — the offer nonce and the hash lock are random; the three folds do not.

```js
// Reproduction: foldTranscript trusts caller-supplied record order.
import { ed25519 } from "@noble/curves/ed25519.js";
import { base58, base64urlnopad } from "@scure/base";
import {
  makeOffer, makeAccept, encodeFrame, generateHashLock,
  foldTranscript, verifyTranscriptRecord, dealRoom, OFFER_ROOM,
} from "./dist/index.js";

const enc = new TextEncoder();
const did = (pk) => "did:key:z" + base58.encode(new Uint8Array([0xed, 0x01, ...pk]));

function party(seedByte) {
  const sk = new Uint8Array(32).fill(seedByte);
  return { sk, did: did(ed25519.getPublicKey(sk)) };
}
const payer = party(1);
const payee = party(2);

// `seq` and `timestampMs` are venue metadata; the signature covers `<room>|<nonce>|<line>`.
let nonceCounter = 1000;
const rec = (room, seq, timestampMs, who, line) => {
  const nonce = String(nonceCounter++);
  const sig = ed25519.sign(enc.encode(`${room}|${nonce}|${line}`), who.sk);
  return { room, seq, timestampMs, sender: who.did, nonce,
           signature: base64urlnopad.encode(sig), line };
};

const T0 = 1_780_000_000_000;
const offer = makeOffer({
  from: payer.did, role: "payer", lock: "hash",
  amount: "1000000", asset: "FLOP", rails: ["paper"],
  claimByMs: T0 + 3_600_000, refundAfterMs: T0 + 7_200_000, expiresMs: T0 + 600_000,
});
const { preimage, hash } = generateHashLock();
const accept = makeAccept(offer, { from: payee.did, statement: hash });
const room = dealRoom(accept.contract);

const lock   = { type: "lock",   from: payer.did, contract: accept.contract, rail: "paper", ref: "paper:1" };
const reveal = { type: "reveal", from: payee.did, contract: accept.contract, ref: "paper:1", secret: preimage };
const refund = { type: "refund", from: payer.did, contract: accept.contract, ref: "paper:1" };

const R = {
  offer:  rec(OFFER_ROOM, 1, T0,                     payer, encodeFrame(offer)),
  accept: rec(OFFER_ROOM, 2, T0 + 60_000,            payee, encodeFrame(accept)),
  lock:   rec(room,       1, T0 + 120_000,           payer, encodeFrame(lock)),
  reveal: rec(room,       2, T0 + 1_800_000,         payee, encodeFrame(reveal)),
  refund: rec(room,       3, T0 + 7_200_000 + 5_000, payer, encodeFrame(refund)),
};

const show = (label, records) => {
  const { state, steps } = foldTranscript(records);
  console.log(`\n${label}`);
  console.log(`  every signature verifies : ${records.every((r) => verifyTranscriptRecord(r).ok)}`);
  console.log(`  seq order as folded      : ${records.map((r) => r.seq).join(",")}`);
  for (const s of steps) {
    console.log(`  [${s.index}] ${String(s.type).padEnd(6)} seq=${s.seq} ok=${String(s.ok).padEnd(5)}${s.reason ? " " + s.reason : ""}`);
  }
  console.log(`  => status: ${state?.status}`);
};

console.log(`contract : ${accept.contract}`);
console.log(`deal room: ${room}`);
show("A. honest order (what actually happened)", [R.offer, R.accept, R.lock, R.reveal, R.refund]);
show("B. same five records, two lines swapped", [R.offer, R.accept, R.lock, R.refund, R.reveal]);
show("C. same records, the reveal row deleted", [R.offer, R.accept, R.lock, R.refund]);
```

§3.5 gives `cancel` the shape `{type, from, contract, reason?}` and allows it "before any lock exists (proposed/accepted)". But the contract id only exists from `accept` on (§3.2: *"From here on every frame names the contract by this id"*). While `proposed` there is nothing for `contract` to name, and the implementation reflects that:

- `tests/tclk.test.ts:277` cancels a proposed offer with `contract: "0x" + "00".repeat(32)`.
- `applyFrame` reads `frame.contract` on a cancel only when `state.status === "accepted"`. While `proposed` the field is not consulted.

Two consequences, both reproducible at `54e5caf`:

1. **A cancel is not bound to an offer.** A reader that folds a room into more than one open contract — the use §2 invites, "run it over every line a stranger wrote" — applies one cancel to every proposed offer from that sender. With offers A and B open, cancelling A with `contract: A.id` (the natural thing to put there) moves B's fold to `cancelled` too.
2. **`receipt{outcome:"cancelled"}` after a proposed-stage cancel can never verify.** The state has `contract === undefined`, so `frame.contract !== state.contract` holds for every 32-byte value. There is no receipt a party can post that the machine accepts, although §3.5 says a receipt follows every terminal outcome.

## Repro (vitest, `tests/`)

```ts
import { describe, expect, it } from "vitest";
import { applyFrame, makeOffer, openContract } from "../src/index.js";

const PAYER = "did:key:z6Mk" + "f".repeat(44); // same fixture spelling as tclk.test.ts
const NOW = 1_756_700_000_000;
const base = {
  from: PAYER, role: "payer" as const, amount: "1000", asset: "USDC", lock: "hash" as const,
  rails: ["flop-htlc"], claimByMs: NOW + 3_600_000, refundAfterMs: NOW + 7_200_000, expiresMs: NOW + 600_000,
};
const a = makeOffer({ ...base, nonce: "aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa" });
const b = makeOffer({ ...base, nonce: "bbbbbbbbbbbbbbbb" });
const cancelA = { type: "cancel" as const, from: PAYER, contract: a.id };

describe("cancel while proposed", () => {
  it("a cancel naming offer A also cancels offer B", () => {
    expect(applyFrame(openContract(b), cancelA, NOW).state.status).toBe("cancelled"); // passes today
  });
  it("no receipt can follow a proposed-stage cancel", () => {
    const cancelled = applyFrame(openContract(a), cancelA, NOW).state;
    expect(cancelled.contract).toBeUndefined();
    const r = applyFrame(cancelled, { type: "receipt", from: PAYER, contract: a.id, outcome: "cancelled" }, NOW);
    expect(r.ok).toBe(false);
    expect(r.reason).toBe("receipt names a different contract"); // passes today
  });
});
```

## Proposal — SPEC and code together, no wire-format change

- **§3.5**: "While `proposed`, `contract` carries the offer `id`; from `accepted` on, the contract id." That is the convention `accept.ref` already uses for the pre-contract handle, so nothing new is introduced.
- **`applyFrame`**: `cancel` while `proposed` requires `frame.contract === state.offer.id`; `receipt` compares against `state.contract ?? state.offer.id`.
- Golden vectors are untouched — no encoding changes. The zero id in the existing test becomes a rejected frame, which is the intended tightening.

If that reading is right I can send the PR with regression tests for both points. Filing as an issue first per CONTRIBUTING, since it touches the state machine.

Running `examples/live-deal.mjs` against `technocore.chat` on 2026-09-02, the run died twice out of three attempts on a transient `503`:

```
Error: kv set tclk-job-10/tiktok-91bf0a1: 503 Service Unavailable
    at Object.set (examples/live-deal.mjs:108:24)
```

`req()` (examples/live-deal.mjs:40-50) loops only on `429`:

```js
const res = await fetch(url, init);
if (res.status !== 429) return res;      // every 5xx returns straight through
```

so the `!res.ok` throws at lines 55, 77, 86 and 108 fire on any transient upstream error. The venue has been returning intermittent `503` under its own load all week — `/rooms` took 8.5s to answer in the same session.

**Why it matters beyond the example:** the walkthrough is what agents will copy. A run that dies between `lock` and `reveal` is not merely inconvenient — on a rail that held value it sits until `refundAfterMs`. The example is the natural place to demonstrate the retry discipline the protocol needs.

**Suggested fix** — same shape as the existing `429` path:

```js
async function req(url, init, what) {
  for (let attempt = 0; ; attempt += 1) {
    const res = await fetch(url, init);
    if (res.status !== 429 && res.status < 500) return res;
    if (attempt >= 3) throw new Error(`${what}: gave up after ${attempt} retries (${res.status})`);
    const stated = Number(res.headers.get("retry-after"));
    const waitMs = res.status === 429
      ? (Number.isFinite(stated) && stated > 0 ? stated : 5) * 1000
      : Math.min(2 ** attempt, 10) * 1000;   // backoff for 5xx
    log("", `${res.status} — waiting ${waitMs / 1000}s`);
    await new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, waitMs));
  }
}
```

Environment: node v22.23.2, `54e5caf`, `npm install && npm run build`, `npx vitest run` → 60 tests, 5 files, all pass.
